    <item>
      <title>Linux Patches Show Promising Results For Lower Latency Of Short Slice Tasks</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/Linux-Latency-Short-Slice-Tasks</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/Linux-Latency-Short-Slice-Tasks</guid>
      <pubDate>Sat, 03 Oct 2026 17:25:38 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="LINUX KERNEL" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/linuxkernel.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; Linaro engineer Vincent Guittot sent out a set of patches on Friday working on scheduling latency improvements, especially when multiple short slice tasks are running concurrently on the same system. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;The set of 18 patches build upon work carried out by Guittot in recent months for addressing different defects in the Linux kernel's scheduler code.  &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Simply put for end users, using the cyclictest and Hackbench benchmarks for stressing the scheduler, Vincent pulled off some very nice improvements especially around the peak latency observed: &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=short_slice_tasks" alt="benchmark results" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Vincent also noted various other improvements too with these various scheduler improvements that range from implementing decay of positive lag to accounting for the slice when selecting the CPU and other adjustments. Those interested in more data and all the fine details about this work can see all the information in full via the &lt;a href="https://lore.kernel.org/linux-pm/20261002154415.2270586-1-vincent.guittot@linaro.org/"&gt;mailing list&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Moose: GNOME Gains Another Local AI App Option</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/GNOME-Moose-Local-AI</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/GNOME-Moose-Local-AI</guid>
      <pubDate>Sat, 03 Oct 2026 11:47:01 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="GNOME" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/gnome.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; Beyond the various local AI agent/chat options not catering to a specific desktop environment, the GNOME desktop has seen &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/search/Newelle"&gt;Newelle&lt;/a&gt; as an AI app catering to GNOME/GTK4. There's now another local AI option for the GNOME desktop with Moose. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Moose is a native GTK and libadwaita-using app for chatting with AI models via Ollama on Linux. Moose can setup Ollama and handle management of the LLMs. Moose supports loading local documents and images and other integration around the GNOME desktop. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=moose" alt="Moose app" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;This third-party Moose app for GNOME was announced today via &lt;a href="https://thisweek.gnome.org/posts/2026/10/twig-268/"&gt;This Week in GNOME&lt;/a&gt;. Moose is available on &lt;a href="https://flathub.org/en/apps/io.github.moooossee.Moose"&gt;Flathub&lt;/a&gt; for those interested while the Rust-based GPLv3 source code is available via &lt;a href="https://github.com/moooossee/moose"&gt;GitHub&lt;/a&gt;. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;This Week in GNOME also highlighted many recent improvements to the GNOME Calendar app to make a better user experience. There is improved calendar management, avoiding the need to restart Calendar, and other improvements. Libadwaita itself also continues seeing improvements. Separately, this week saw the announcement of &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/Toolpak"&gt;the new Toolpak project&lt;/a&gt; for image-based systems.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>KosmicKrisp Now Conformant To Vulkan 1.4, Improving Performance Against Apple's Metal</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/KosmicKrisp-Vulkan-1.4-2026</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/KosmicKrisp-Vulkan-1.4-2026</guid>
      <pubDate>Sat, 03 Oct 2026 11:24:07 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="VULKAN" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/vulkan.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/search/KosmicKrisp"&gt;KosmicKrisp&lt;/a&gt; is the effort led by LunarG for developing a modern Vulkan-on-Metal driver for Apple systems within Mesa. This modern alternative to MoltenVK continues progressing well and now enjoys Vulkan 1.4 conformance and its performance continues inching closer to that of the native Apple Metal API. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Aitor Camacho Larrondo of LunarG presented at XDC2026 Toronto this week around KosmicKrisp and its latest developments, which most notably is KosmicKrisp passing the Vulkan 1.4 CTS and being deemed a conformant implementation by The Khronos Group. KosmicKrisp is considered by LunarG to be production ready on modern macOS and using the Apple Silicon hardware for Vulkan API support atop Metal. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=kosmickrisp_1_med" alt="KosmicKrisp Vulkan 1.4" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Excitingly, its performance is in good shape to the alternative MoltenVK Vulkan-on-Metal implementation and not too far behind native Metal performance. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=kosmickrisp_2_med" alt="KosmicKrisp performance" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;LunarG is continuing to invest in enhancing the KosmicKrisp performance as well as weeding out remaining bugs.  Other Vulkan features are expected to be implemented as well as enhancing the tooling support around this Vulkan API implementation for Macs. KosmicKrisp to date has also only been focused on macOS and not iOS. More resources are needed before KosmicKrisp on iOS could really become a reality. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;iframe width="560" height="315" src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/TPwe53kCJuM?si=Kl8sUx3tyOSBH28f&amp;amp;start=28788" title="YouTube video player" frameborder="0" allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share" referrerpolicy="strict-origin-when-cross-origin" allowfullscreen&gt;&lt;/iframe&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Those interested can learn more about the 2026 state of KosmicKrisp via the XDC presentation embedded above as well as the &lt;a href="https://indico.freedesktop.org/event/12/contributions/538/attachments/377/541/KosmicKrisp_Production_Ready.pdf"&gt;slides&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Linux 7.4 To Support The Raspberry Pi 10-Inch Touch Display 2</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/Linux-74-RPi-10-Touch-Display-2</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/Linux-74-RPi-10-Touch-Display-2</guid>
      <pubDate>Sat, 03 Oct 2026 10:33:10 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="RASPBERRY PI" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/raspberrypi.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; We are nearing the cut-off of new feature material being accepted to DRM-Next ahead of the Linux 7.4 merge window. But this week another round of DRM-Misc changes made it, which included some new hardware support and other last minute items.  &lt;br /&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;Most notable with the DRM-Misc-Next pull request this week is adding support for the &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/10-inch-Raspberry-Pi-Touch-2"&gt;Raspberry Pi 10" Touch Display 2&lt;/a&gt; hardware that launched this summer. This 10-inch Raspberry Pi touch display will now play nicely with the mainline kernel beginning on Linux 7.4. The Device Tree additions are now in place for that 10" Raspberry Pi Touch Display 2 MIPI-DSI TFT LCD panel with Ilitek IL79600A controller.  &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=raspberry_pi_touch_10_1" alt="Raspberry Pi Touch Display 2" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;The 10-inch Raspberry Pi Touch Display 2 has a 1200 x 1920 resolution, multi-touch input, and retails for around $80 USD.  &lt;br /&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;The DRM-Misc-Next pull request also adds &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/AMD-Gorgon-Halo-NPU-LInux"&gt;support for the NPU on the Ryzen AI Max 400 series "Gorgon Halo"&lt;/a&gt; after it was seemingly forgotten about until now. Just a new device ID is needed for that AIE2 Revision 9 NPU.  &lt;br /&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;The Renesas DRM driver also adds support for the RZ/G3L SoC. That about rounds out the interesting material in this week's &lt;a href="https://lore.kernel.org/dri-devel/20261001120401.GA491855@linux.fritz.box/"&gt;drm-misc-next pull&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Linux 7.3 To Better Support The High-End Turtle Beach VelocityOne Race</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/Linux-7.3-TB-VelocityOne-Race</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/Linux-7.3-TB-VelocityOne-Race</guid>
      <pubDate>Sat, 03 Oct 2026 10:18:05 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="HARDWARE" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/hardware.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; Coming with Sunday's Linux 7.3-rc6 kernel release is support added for the Turtle Beach VelocityOne Race, a high-end $649~700 USD racing wheel and pedal system. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Turtle Beach doesn't officially support Linux with their products like the VelocityOne Race wheel and pedal kit for racing simulators. Basic HID input has worked but with reported issues. Coming now for Linux 7.3 is adding the Turtle Beach VelocityOne Race to the &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/Linux-hid-universal-pidff"&gt;hid-universal-pidff driver&lt;/a&gt;. This is the driver that came about last year for improving device support for the Linux simracing community. This driver has already helped work out issues with racing wheels/pedals of other brands while now the Turtle Beach VelocityOne Race stands to benefit too. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=velocityone_race_med" alt="VelocityOne Race" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;As the hid-universal-pidff driver just needs the vendor and device IDs for the VelocityOne Race kit, it was safe for merging during this late stage of the Linux 7.3 kernel development. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;The support was sent into the kernel as part of this week's &lt;a href="https://lore.kernel.org/lkml/ar9lU7uxSwvV57e9@beelink/"&gt;HID pull&lt;/a&gt;. That pull also fixes some buffer overflows in the Intel THC driver, some fixes for Sashiko AI findings, and also goes ahead and &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/Linux-Reverting-Logitech-Bolt"&gt;reverts the Logitech Bolt Receiver HID++ support due to too many bugs&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>KDE Plasma 6.8 Now Makes Tiled Windows Fit Together More Nicely</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/KDE-Plasma-6.8-Tiled-Windows</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/KDE-Plasma-6.8-Tiled-Windows</guid>
      <pubDate>Sat, 03 Oct 2026 10:07:10 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="KDE" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/kde.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; There is just over one week to go until the much anticipated KDE Plasma 6.8 desktop release. Some last minute fixes continue flowing into Plasma 6.8 as well as early feature work continuing for Plasma 6.9. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Making it in time for the Plasma 6.8 release is that tiled windows now have square corners so they fit together nicely with other windows. This stems from a &lt;a href="https://bugs.kde.org/show_bug.cgi?id=490716"&gt;2024 bug report&lt;/a&gt; that rounded corners should be disabled when the window is tiling , similar to how Microsoft Windows behaves. It was noted KDE Plasma used to behave like this bug regressed at some point. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;As shown in This Week in Plasma the result of the change for tiled windows with KDE Plasma 6.8: &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=kde_tiled_windows" alt="KDE tiled windows with non-rounded corners" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;For Plasma 6.7.6 meanwhile is a fix where KWin could crash when using the NVIDIA 610.57.04 graphics driver or newer. There is also another crash fix for Plasma when dragging a widget to a panel. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Plasma 6.8 also has a crash fix in response to apps closing or crashing in very specific ways. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Also fixed for Plasma 6.8 is remote desktop authentication working with the FreeRDP 3.32 library and newer. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;One other last minute change for Plasma 6.8 is having the System Monitor and its widgets measure GPU memory usage more accurately. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;More details on these changes can be found via &lt;a href="https://blogs.kde.org/2026/10/03/this-week-in-plasma-6.8-beta-2-keep-testing/"&gt;This Week in Plasma&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Wine 11.19 Released With Wayland Color Management For Vulkan, DNS Query Caching</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/Wine-11.19-Released</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/Wine-11.19-Released</guid>
      <pubDate>Sat, 03 Oct 2026 00:29:10 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="WINE" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/wine.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; We are nearing the point of the Wine 12.0 stable release coming up in early 2027 while for now the Wine 11.xx bi-weekly development releases continue. Out today is Wine 11.19 with a few new features and plenty of fixes. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Wine 11.19 introduces support for vertical text within the GDIPlus code. Also on the character side is support for Unicode 18.0 characters. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;This new development release also now supports caching of DNS queries and improves the VBScript parser. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;One change not mentioned in the Wine 11.19 release highlights but worth calling out is the Wine Wayland driver now implementing the color management protocol for the Vulkan color space. This improves color accuracy for Windows games/apps running on Wayland. More details in &lt;a href="https://gitlab.winehq.org/wine/wine/-/merge_requests/12224"&gt;this merge request&lt;/a&gt;. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;On the fixes side, Wine 11.19 has 23 known bug fixes affecting various games and other apps. Downloads and the full list of Wine 11.19 changes over the past two weeks via &lt;a href="https://gitlab.winehq.org/wine/wine/-/releases/wine-11.19"&gt;WineHQ.org&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Linux 7.4 To Introduce Initial Device Trees For Apple M4, A18 Pro For MacBook Neo</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/Linux-7.4-Apple-Device-Trees</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/Linux-7.4-Apple-Device-Trees</guid>
      <pubDate>Fri, 02 Oct 2026 20:32:41 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="APPLE" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/apple.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; Asahi Linux developer Sven Peter today sent out the pull requests of the Apple SoC Device Tree changes they are ready to upstream for the Linux 7.4 merge window happening later this month. Most notable is the initial Device Tree for Apple systems using the base M4 SoC model as well as for the MacBook Neo with the A18 Pro SoC. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;The initial Device Trees for Apple Macs using the base M4 SoC as well as the MacBook Neo with its A18 Pro will debut in Linux 7.4. But as shown in recent kernels with the initial Apple M3 DTs, it doesn't yet mean it will be a usable experience for Linux users wanting to run the mainline kernel on these newer Macs. It's good enough to begin booting the Linux kernel but not much more when it comes to the GPU and other functionality still to be enabled. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;The M4 Device Trees contain the "minimal amount of hardware" and what's contained is for hardware blocks that are largely unchanged between the M3 and M4 generations. Devices brought up include the CPU cores, interrupt controller, power states, watchdog, serial, pin controller, i2c, and the frame-buffer. All the rest of the functionality will be left to future post-7.4 Linux kernel versions. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;The M4 Device Trees are for the MacBook Pro (14-inch, M4, 2024), iMac (24-inch, 2x USB-C, M4, 2024), iMac (24-inch, 4x USB-C, M4, 2024), MacBook Air (13-inch, M4, 2025), MacBook Air (15-inch, M4, 2025), and Mac Mini (M4, 2024). &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=macbook_neo_1_med" alt="MacBook Neo" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;For the MacBook Neo it's fairly similar to Apple M4 series devices but using the A-Series SoC. Like the M4 support, it's going to be very limited for Linux 7.4 and just to encourage future development. This initial bring-up includes the CPU cores, interrupt controller, watchdog, serial, and frame-buffer. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;These initial DTs are found with &lt;a href="https://lore.kernel.org/lkml/20261002190232.6449-1-sven@kernel.org/"&gt;this pull request&lt;/a&gt; ahead of the Linux 7.4 merge window. Sven Peter also sent out &lt;a href="https://lore.kernel.org/lkml/20261002190958.8205-1-sven@kernel.org/"&gt;another pull request&lt;/a&gt; for Linux 7.4 with shared GPIO support and RTKit crashlog support for older 32-bit co-processors.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Intel Ready With A Big Improvement For Battlemage With Linux 7.4</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/Intel-CPU-Binds-ULLS-Migration</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/Intel-CPU-Binds-ULLS-Migration</guid>
      <pubDate>Fri, 02 Oct 2026 18:34:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="INTEL" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/intel.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; Sent out on Thursday was the last round of planned Intel Xe kernel driver improvements targeting the upcoming Linux 7.4 cycle. There is one big improvement that is set to benefit Intel discrete GPUs with at least Battlemage benefiting nicely. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;The major addition for the Intel Xe driver this round is implementing CPU binds and ULLS on the migration queue. While it may not sound like some enticing feature, it does come with some rather sizable performance benefits. Intel engineer Matthew Brost explained in &lt;a href="https://lore.kernel.org/all/20260925045320.1325860-1-matthew.brost@intel.com/"&gt;the patch series&lt;/a&gt; cover letter: &lt;br /&gt;&lt;blockquote&gt;"On BMG, measurements show that when the GPU is continuously processing faults, copy jobs run approximately 30–40µs faster (depending on the test case) with ULLS compared to traditional GuC submission with SLPC enabled on the migration queue. Startup from a cold GPU shows an even larger speedup. Given the critical nature of fault performance, ULLS appears to be a worthwhile feature. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;In addition to driver telemetry, UMD compute benchmarks consistently show multiple GB/s improvement in pagefault benchmarks with ULLS enabled. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;ULLS will consume more power (not yet measured) due to a continuously running batch on the paging engine. However, compute UMDs already do this on engines exposed to users, so this seems like a worthwhile tradeoff. To mitigate power concerns, ULLS will exit after a period of time in which no faults have been processed. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;CPU binds are required for ULLS to function, as the migration queue needs exclusive access to the paging hardware engine. Thus, CPU binds are included here. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Beyond being a requirement for ULLS, CPU binds should also reduce VM-bind latency, provide clearer multi-tile and TLB-invalidation layering, reduce pressure on GuC during fault storms as it is bypassed, and decouple kernel binds from unrelated copy/clear jobs—especially beneficial when faults are serviced in parallel. In a parallel-faulting test case, average bind time was reduced by approximately 15µs. In the worst case, 2MB copy time (~60–140µs) × (number of pagefault threads − 1) of latency would otherwise be added to a single fault. Reducing this latency increases overall throughput of the fault handler."&lt;/blockquote&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Overall this ULLS for the migration queue plus CPU binds appears to be a nice win in the end. ULLS in this context is the &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/search/Ultra+Low+Latency+Scheduling"&gt;Ultra Low Latency Scheduling&lt;/a&gt;. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=intel-arc-pro-b70-four&amp;image=arc_pro_b70_2_med" alt="Intel Arc Pro B70 Battlemage" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;This new Xe pull request also includes a fix for ULLS for Crescent Island. The pull also includes a new Panther Lake device ID of 0xB0A1. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;&lt;a href="https://lore.kernel.org/dri-devel/ar7JQUI6b+PcQa4S@gsse-cloud1.jf.intel.com/"&gt;The pull&lt;/a&gt; is on its way to DRM-Next for queuing ahead of the Linux 7.4 merge window later in October. Linux 7.4 stable in turn should be out around late December.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Ubuntu Working On SHA3 For Debian's APT For If/When SHA2 Is Broken</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/Ubuntu-SHA3-For-Debian-APT</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/Ubuntu-SHA3-For-Debian-APT</guid>
      <pubDate>Fri, 02 Oct 2026 18:10:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="DEBIAN" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/debian.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; Ubuntu developer Gianpiero Carpinelli at Canonical has been working on introducing SHA3-256 and SHA3-384 support for Debian's APT packaging tool in preparing for if/when that SHA2 is broken. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;There's been several mentions recently in various developer updates around Canonical working on SHA3 support for APT and this week it culminated with an open pull request for getting this SHA3-256 and SHA3-384 support into upstream APT. This allows SHA3 as an alternative to SHA2 in Release, Packages, and Sources when using new SHA3 options but not engaged by default. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;The motivation is explained in the pull request over concern that the SHA2 standard could be broken before Ubuntu 24.04 LTS support ends in the year 2036. So to prepare for that likely eventuality, they are working now on getting SHA3 support rolled out and back-ported to Ubuntu 24.04 LTS so that they can be prepared should they ever need to change for better security: &lt;br /&gt;&lt;blockquote&gt;"Motivation: Ubuntu 24.04 is supported for ten to twelve years. If SHA-2 is broken in that window, clients already deployed must understand a structurally different hash family so archives can move to it. OpenPGP signature digests are out of scope. &lt;br /&gt;"&lt;/blockquote&gt; &lt;br /&gt;So far SHA2 has not been broken but there have been theoretical attacks that aren't applicable to real-world implementations. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=apt_sha3" alt="APT SHA3 merge request" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Those interested in this SHA3 support being worked on for APT can see &lt;a href="https://salsa.debian.org/apt-team/apt/-/merge_requests/626"&gt;the Debian.org APT merge request&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
